umeshcore for Debian
====================

The service is installed but deliberately NOT enabled
-----------------------------------------------------

Starting this daemon puts a 22 dBm PA on the air. Which band you may transmit
on is a legal question, and transmitting into an unfitted antenna connector
will damage the radio, so installing the package never starts it. Review the
configuration first:

    sudoedit /etc/umeshcore/umeshcored.ini

`lora_freq` is required and has no built-in default; the shipped file is set
to 869.618 (EU868). Set the plan for your region, fit an antenna, then:

    sudo systemctl enable --now umeshcored
    journalctl -u umeshcored -f

Upgrading the package restarts the daemon if you enabled it, and leaves it
alone if you did not.

Before the first start
----------------------

1. Enable SPI. The AIO v2 puts the SX1262 on SPI1, so add to
   /boot/firmware/config.txt and reboot:

       dtparam=spi=on
       dtoverlay=spi1-1cs

   Confirm /dev/spidev1.0 exists afterwards. The unit has
   ConditionPathExists=/dev/spidev1.0 and will refuse to start without it.

2. Turn on the LoRa power rail (GPIO16). The daemon does not switch it -- it
   is board-level state owned by whatever manages the uConsole's power. Until
   it is high the SX1262 is unpowered; the daemon starts anyway and connects
   to the radio on its next retry (lora_retry_interval, 10 seconds).

3. Stop meshtasticd if it is installed -- it holds the same GPIO lines and
   umeshcored will fail with EBUSY.

The package creates a system user `umeshcore` and a system group `meshcore`.
The udev rules in /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/99-umeshcore.rules grant that group
access to the SPI bus and the GPIO character devices. Adding yourself to
`meshcore` lets you run the daemon by hand for debugging:

    sudo adduser "$USER" meshcore     # log out and back in

State, and what purging does not remove
---------------------------------------

On first start the daemon generates an Ed25519 identity and writes it to
/var/lib/umeshcore/identity (mode 0600). That file *is* your node: back it up.
Deleting it gives you a new public key and every contact will see you as a
stranger.

For that reason purging the package leaves /var/lib/umeshcore, the `umeshcore`
user and the `meshcore` group in place. Remove them by hand if you really mean
to discard the node identity:

    sudo rm -rf /var/lib/umeshcore
    sudo deluser umeshcore
    sudo delgroup meshcore

Companion protocol access
-------------------------

The companion protocol has no authentication at all -- anyone who can reach
the port can read your messages and send as you. `companion_bind` therefore
defaults to 127.0.0.1. Only widen it on a network you control, ideally behind
an SSH tunnel or WireGuard.

